  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Philadelphia came to the Bronx and left with a statement. A 7-run margin that said they were the better team. Will Warren's early exit set the tone, and once Texas led by four in the third, the Yankees never mounted anything resembling a threat. Ben Rice hit one solo shot in a losing effort. Thirteen strikeouts and two runs on ten hits is not offense. It is an indictment.",
    "what_this_means": "The game decided itself early, but the real problem is what it exposed. Warren gave up six runs in three innings, and the bullpen. Hill, Doval, and Yarbrough spanning five innings. Held Philadelphia scoreless after that. Clean relief. Wasted on a lineup that couldn't string together a meaningful sequence all night. They scored once with runners in scoring position; they had ten opportunities. That's the margin between holding tight and getting blown out by seven. They had the chances. They didn't convert them when it mattered.",
    "what_to_watch": "Noah Schultz takes the mound for Chicago tonight at 7:40 PM. He's carried a 6.04 ERA into this matchup. An opportunity the Yankees lineup cannot afford to waste the way they did against a Philadelphia team that proved it can run away with a game in one inning.",
